### Description

The new API would look similar to `collect`. Eg.
```python
lf = pl.LazyFrame(
    {
        "a": ["a", "b", "a", "b", "b", "c"],
        "b": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6],
        "c": [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1],
    }
)
q = lf.group_by("a", maintain_order=True).agg(pl.all().sum()).sort(
    "a"
)
df, df_times = lf.profile(engine="gpu") # or GPU configuration object
```

If we allow the profile functions in the Polars rust layer to accept a callback (in the same way we do for collect), we can get the timing information. We'd need to change this [profile function](https://github.com/pola-rs/polars/tree/main/crates/polars-python/src/lazyframe) in the rust layer like
```rust
// PyLazyFrame::profile
fn profile(&self, py: Python, lambda_post_opt: Option<PyObject>) -> PyResult<(PyDataFrame, PyDataFrame)> {
    // follow the logic in collect
}
```
And we'd need to add an engine kwarg to profile function in polars.
```python
def profile(
    self,
    *,
    ...
    engine: EngineType = "cpu",
) -> tuple[DataFrame, DataFrame]:
    ...
    # Following the logic in collect
```
We'd would also need to somehow pass a callable from rust to python that emulates [`state.node_timer.store`](https://github.com/pola-rs/polars/blob/main/crates/polars-expr/src/state/node_timer.rs#L19), so we can time the nodes correctly. For example, in [python_scan.rs](https://github.com/pola-rs/polars/blob/74b992569b7b0ec963fcf6e34d1e3c894d74f246/crates/polars-mem-engine/src/executors/scan/python_scan.rs#L84C1-L91C61), we'd want
```rust
let args = (
    python_scan_function,
    with_columns.map(|x| x.into_iter().map(|x| x.to_string()).collect::<Vec<_>>()),
    predicate,
    n_rows,
    batch_size,
    state.node_timer.store, // <<< add this
);
callable.call1(args).map_err(to_compute_err)
```
And we'd make the `node_timer` bridge to in pyo3 look something like
```rust
#[pyclass]
struct NodeTimer {
    inner: Option<NodeTimer>
}

#[pymethods]
impl NodeTimer {
    fn __call__(&self, py: Python<'_>, start: StartInstant, end: EndInstant, name: String) {
        ...
    }
}
```

Any advice or comments on the approach is appreciated?

cc. @wence-
